WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.602
Here is the workflow I want you to use whenever you build a join.

00:00:03.602 --> 00:00:07.602
Start by defining the grain.

00:00:07.602 --> 00:00:14.372
Before writing SQL, say in plain language what one row in the final result should represent.

00:00:14.372 --> 00:00:19.612
Maybe the target is one patient, one admission, one student registration, or one sales transaction.

00:00:19.612 --> 00:00:23.227
Next, identify the row sets you are combining.

00:00:23.227 --> 00:00:25.452
Then define the matching rule.

00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:31.032
Usually the relationship uses a key such as patient ID, customer ID, or course ID.

00:00:31.032 --> 00:00:36.957
Do not assume that columns should be joined just because they have similar names.

00:00:36.957 --> 00:00:39.862
The relationship should make sense in the data model.

00:00:39.862 --> 00:00:44.187
After that, decide which unmatched rows must remain.

00:00:44.187 --> 00:00:49.777
If the requirement says every patient, then the patient population must be preserved.

00:00:49.777 --> 00:00:54.887
If the requirement says only patients with admissions, then a match is required.

00:00:54.887 --> 00:01:03.277
Then predict cardinality. Ask whether each row can match zero rows, one row, or many rows on the other side.

00:01:03.277 --> 00:01:05.872
Finally, validate the output.

00:01:05.872 --> 00:01:11.222
Compare your predicted result with the actual row count and inspect a few known cases.

00:01:11.222 --> 00:01:16.122
The core habit is simple: start from the business rule, not from a JOIN keyword.
